 "result_text": "I fixed the crash: pyflakes no longer raises an internal error on `__all__, = (\"fizz\", \"buzz\")`. Running `python -m pyflakes` on that exact file now prints nothing, and the existing test suite passes (714 passed, 44 skipped). I didn't add a test, since you said not to touch tests.\n\nThe cause was in `pyflakes/checker.py`. Pyflakes treated any binding named `__all__` at module level as an export list. With `__all__, = ...`, the name's parent node is a tuple of targets, not the assignment, so `ExportBinding` looked for `.value` on a `Tuple` and failed.\n\nThe check now only creates an `ExportBinding` when `__all__` is the direct target of an `Assign`, `AugAssign` or `AnnAssign`. Anything else, such as the tuple-unpacking form, is treated as an ordinary binding.\n\nThe issue suggested reporting this form as invalid to the user. I didn't add a warning. The unpacked `__all__` is just not tracked as an export list, so the names `fizz` and `buzz` aren't checked against it.",
